1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,999
A colleague calls you over. Their terminal
shows a wall of hints ending in "Could

2
00:00:09,039 --> 00:00:13,370
not apply". git branch prints the line
on screen. git status says "interactive

3
00:00:13,410 --> 00:00:16,788
rebase in progress", and they're
certain they never typed -i.

4
00:00:16,828 --> 00:00:21,439
They ask two things: "Have I broken the
branch?" and "Is it safe to delete whatever

5
00:00:21,479 --> 00:00:21,999
is stuck?"

6
00:00:22,339 --> 00:00:26,619
The answer to the first is no, and
you can prove it with one command.

7
00:00:26,659 --> 00:00:31,814
The answer to the second is also no, and
the reason is a directory inside .git that

8
00:00:31,854 --> 00:00:36,384
almost nobody has opened. Today you
open it. Keep both questions in mind.

9
00:00:36,424 --> 00:00:38,262
Each gets its proof on screen.

10
00:00:40,102 --> 00:00:43,734
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:43,774 --> 00:00:48,731
A rebase, remember, copies your branch's
commits onto a new base and then moves the

12
00:00:48,771 --> 00:00:53,607
branch. In the last video one ran from
start to finish in one second, and you saw

13
00:00:53,647 --> 00:00:58,603
only the before and the after. To see the
machinery you have to make it stop in the

14
00:00:58,643 --> 00:00:59,024
middle.

15
00:00:59,364 --> 00:01:04,035
So today's repository has a conflict built
in: the second commit of the feature

16
00:01:04,075 --> 00:01:08,925
branch changes a line that main has also
changed, and Git can't combine the two by

17
00:01:08,965 --> 00:01:13,099
itself. The rebase replays one commit,
stops at the second, and waits.

18
00:01:13,139 --> 00:01:16,199
The conflict itself is the
subject of a later video.

19
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:21,089
Today you leave it alone and look around:
where HEAD is, where the branch is, what

20
00:01:21,129 --> 00:01:25,919
the state directory contains, and what the
special refs ORIG_HEAD and REBASE_HEAD

21
00:01:25,959 --> 00:01:26,217
name.

22
00:01:26,557 --> 00:01:31,496
Then you leave with --abort, read the
reflogs, and take a short look at the older

23
00:01:31,536 --> 00:01:36,536
implementation, the apply backend, because
you'll meet its messages in old scripts

24
00:01:36,576 --> 00:01:37,519
and old answers.

25
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:40,772
After this video you can:

26
00:01:41,112 --> 00:01:45,995
Say where HEAD, the branch ref, ORIG_HEAD
and REBASE_HEAD point while a rebase is

27
00:01:46,035 --> 00:01:46,481
stopped.

28
00:01:46,821 --> 00:01:51,507
Read the files of .git/rebase-merge to
learn what is done and what remains.

29
00:01:51,847 --> 00:01:56,424
Explain when the branch ref moves, and what
that means for commits made during the

30
00:01:56,464 --> 00:01:56,706
stop.

31
00:01:57,046 --> 00:01:59,707
Contrast the merge backend
with the apply backend.

32
00:02:01,547 --> 00:02:06,541
In one sentence: a rebase is a small
interpreter. It writes a list of instructions

33
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:11,635
into a state directory inside .git, executes
them one at a time on a detached HEAD,

34
00:02:11,675 --> 00:02:15,011
and touches the branch ref
only when the list is empty.

35
00:02:15,351 --> 00:02:18,581
Each clause of that sentence
is something you can check.

36
00:02:18,621 --> 00:02:23,486
On screen is today's repository before the
rebase: three commits on feat/rerank, and

37
00:02:23,526 --> 00:02:24,829
one new commit on main.

38
00:02:25,169 --> 00:02:29,360
"A list of instructions": one line
per commit, each beginning with pick.

39
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,885
Git calls it the todo list, and
the file is git-rebase-todo.

40
00:02:33,225 --> 00:02:38,414
"A state directory": .git/rebase-merge/. It
exists exactly as long as a rebase is in

41
00:02:38,454 --> 00:02:43,517
progress. It records which ref will be
moved at the end, where that ref pointed at

42
00:02:43,557 --> 00:02:48,248
the start, which commit the copies are
built on, what has been done and what

43
00:02:48,288 --> 00:02:48,746
remains.

44
00:02:49,086 --> 00:02:54,324
"On a detached HEAD": while the rebase
runs, .git/HEAD holds a raw commit ID, not

45
00:02:54,364 --> 00:02:58,950
the name of a branch. HEAD walks along
the new commits as they're made.

46
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:04,288
"Touches the branch ref only when the list
is empty": this is the clause people get

47
00:03:04,328 --> 00:03:07,808
wrong. While a rebase is stopped,
the branch hasn't moved.

48
00:03:07,848 --> 00:03:12,603
It still names the old tip. The new commits
made so far are reachable only from

49
00:03:12,643 --> 00:03:12,906
HEAD.

50
00:03:13,246 --> 00:03:18,226
Two consequences. First, --abort is cheap
and safe: it only has to attach HEAD to

51
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:22,255
the branch again, and restore the
index and working tree from it.

52
00:03:22,295 --> 00:03:26,965
Second, if you make commits of your own
during the stop, they're made on the

53
00:03:27,005 --> 00:03:31,551
detached HEAD. They become part of the
branch only if the rebase finishes.

54
00:03:31,891 --> 00:03:36,374
The special names. ORIG_HEAD is the tip
of the branch before the rebase began.

55
00:03:36,414 --> 00:03:41,187
REBASE_HEAD is the commit that's being
replayed right now, and it exists only while

56
00:03:41,227 --> 00:03:42,347
a rebase is stopped.

57
00:03:42,687 --> 00:03:45,948
The textbook attaches a
version note to those names.

58
00:03:45,988 --> 00:03:50,900
Older behavior: the glossary called every
such file, ORIG_HEAD and REBASE_HEAD

59
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,138
included, a pseudoref, and
most tutorials still do.

60
00:03:54,178 --> 00:03:59,407
Current behavior, since Git 2.46: "pseudoref"
means only FETCH_HEAD and MERGE_HEAD,

61
00:03:59,447 --> 00:04:04,232
and the others are ordinary refs that
live at the root of the ref namespace.

62
00:04:04,272 --> 00:04:09,057
Recommended: say "root ref", and read
them with git rev-parse, not with cat.

63
00:04:09,397 --> 00:04:12,735
And the failure mode: deleting
the state directory by hand.

64
00:04:12,775 --> 00:04:15,599
That leaves HEAD detached
on a half-built history.

65
00:04:17,439 --> 00:04:21,131
A picture helps. Think of a tiny
interpreter running a script.

66
00:04:21,171 --> 00:04:26,127
The script is the todo list. done is the
part already executed, git-rebase-todo the

67
00:04:26,167 --> 00:04:31,064
part still to run, and msgnum and end are
the program counter: instruction 2 of 3.

68
00:04:31,104 --> 00:04:33,953
A conflict is the interpreter
pausing for input.

69
00:04:34,293 --> 00:04:37,679
The move of the branch isn't
something that happens along the way.

70
00:04:37,719 --> 00:04:40,482
It's the last step of the
script, after the last pick.

71
00:04:40,822 --> 00:04:45,617
Where the picture breaks: a paused program
usually holds its state in memory, and

72
00:04:45,657 --> 00:04:49,139
killing the process loses it.
Git holds the state in files.

73
00:04:49,179 --> 00:04:53,914
You can close the terminal, restart the
machine, come back the next day, and git

74
00:04:53,954 --> 00:04:57,138
status will read the same
state directory back to you.

75
00:04:57,178 --> 00:05:01,913
That's also why a rebase left half done
by somebody else is still sitting there,

76
00:05:01,953 --> 00:05:02,391
waiting.

77
00:05:04,231 --> 00:05:10,714
This is the picture to keep. HEAD is detached
on 1c9f69a, the one copy made so far.

78
00:05:10,754 --> 00:05:15,351
feat/rerank and ORIG_HEAD are
both on the old tip, ad106e3.

79
00:05:15,391 --> 00:05:19,831
And REBASE_HEAD marks 569e6c9,
the commit being replayed.

80
00:05:20,171 --> 00:05:24,721
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper:
copy this graph with its four labels.

81
00:05:24,761 --> 00:05:28,149
You'll check each one against
the terminal in step five.

82
00:05:28,189 --> 00:05:30,352
Say the labels out loud as you draw.

83
00:05:35,192 --> 00:05:38,227
And this is what the files
of the state directory mean.

84
00:05:38,267 --> 00:05:40,071
The demo opens the ones you need.

85
00:05:41,911 --> 00:05:43,407
Step 1: before.

86
00:05:43,747 --> 00:05:46,919
Three commits on feat/rerank,
one new commit on main.

87
00:05:46,959 --> 00:05:51,769
The middle commit, "Fetch 20 candidates
for the reranker", touches the line that

88
00:05:51,809 --> 00:05:52,557
main changed.

89
00:05:52,897 --> 00:05:55,857
Step 2: the stop. git rebase 🟡 CAUTION.

90
00:05:56,197 --> 00:06:01,136
One commit replayed, the second stopped
with a conflict, exit status 1.

91
00:06:01,176 --> 00:06:06,957
Now the question of this video, as a quick
quiz. Where does feat/rerank point right

92
00:06:06,997 --> 00:06:10,954
now? A, at the old tip. B, at
the one copy that was made.

93
00:06:10,994 --> 00:06:13,129
C, somewhere else. Your answer?

94
00:06:17,969 --> 00:06:19,215
Step 3: HEAD.

95
00:06:19,555 --> 00:06:24,206
.git/HEAD holds a raw commit
ID, not ref: refs/heads/....

96
00:06:24,246 --> 00:06:30,791
HEAD is detached. It sits on 1c9f69a, the
copy of "Add reranker skeleton", which

97
00:06:30,831 --> 00:06:37,622
sits on the tip of main. git branch describes
the situation as "no branch, rebasing

98
00:06:37,662 --> 00:06:38,692
feat/rerank".

99
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:41,098
Step 4: the state directory.

100
00:06:41,438 --> 00:06:43,977
Sixteen files. You need six of them.

101
00:06:44,317 --> 00:06:48,798
head-name: the ref that will be moved at
the end. orig-head: where it pointed at the

102
00:06:48,838 --> 00:06:53,211
start. onto: what the copies are built on.
done: two picks, the one that succeeded

103
00:06:53,251 --> 00:06:56,279
and the one that stopped.
git-rebase-todo: one pick left.

104
00:06:56,319 --> 00:07:00,639
msgnum and end: 2 of 3. And author-script:
the author name, email and date of the

105
00:07:00,679 --> 00:07:04,998
commit being replayed, to be reused for
its copy. That file is how a rebase keeps

106
00:07:05,038 --> 00:07:07,151
the author while changing the committer.

107
00:07:07,491 --> 00:07:12,150
Step 5: the four names. HEAD, the
branch, ORIG_HEAD and REBASE_HEAD.

108
00:07:12,190 --> 00:07:15,951
Take out your drawing, and
predict which two are equal.

109
00:07:15,991 --> 00:07:17,817
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

110
00:07:22,657 --> 00:07:28,246
Four names, three commits. ORIG_HEAD
is ad106e3, the tip before the rebase.

111
00:07:28,286 --> 00:07:34,324
REBASE_HEAD is 569e6c9, the commit being
replayed. HEAD is 1c9f69a, the last copy

112
00:07:34,364 --> 00:07:38,827
made. And feat/rerank is ad106e3:
it still equals ORIG_HEAD.

113
00:07:38,867 --> 00:07:44,980
The branch hasn't moved. That's the answer
to the quiz, A, and to your colleague's

114
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:50,083
first question: no, the branch isn't
broken. It hasn't been touched.

115
00:07:50,423 --> 00:07:52,326
Step 6: status.

116
00:07:52,666 --> 00:07:57,413
git status reads the state directory back
to you: what was done, what remains, and

117
00:07:57,453 --> 00:08:01,908
the three ways out. And the first line
says "interactive rebase in progress",

118
00:08:01,948 --> 00:08:06,753
although no -i was typed. The root cause,
from the textbook: the default backend of

119
00:08:06,793 --> 00:08:10,840
git rebase is the machinery that once
served only interactive rebases.

120
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:15,685
It always writes the marker file interactive,
and git status reports what it finds.

121
00:08:16,025 --> 00:08:20,983
Step 7: leave the way you came. git rebase
--abort 🟡 CAUTION in the textbook's

122
00:08:21,023 --> 00:08:24,764
table, with one thing it can't
give back. The five answers.

123
00:08:24,804 --> 00:08:29,570
It changes HEAD, the index and the
working tree: all return to the old tip.

124
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:34,696
It destroys resolution work in progress,
the edits you made to conflicted files.

125
00:08:35,036 --> 00:08:39,904
Preview with git status. There's no recovery
for unstaged resolution edits, and the

126
00:08:39,944 --> 00:08:44,871
copies already made stay in the reflog. It's
appropriate whenever you want the state

127
00:08:44,911 --> 00:08:48,420
before the rebase and have no
resolution work worth keeping.

128
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:50,253
Here nothing has been resolved.

129
00:08:50,593 --> 00:08:55,191
On the branch again, clean. The
reflog shows start, one pick, abort.

130
00:08:55,231 --> 00:08:59,624
The copy 1c9f69a is now reachable
from nothing except the reflog.

131
00:08:59,964 --> 00:09:04,638
Look at the column "Current branch
ref". Unchanged, unchanged, unchanged.

132
00:09:04,678 --> 00:09:10,062
And look at --quit: it removes the state
directory and leaves HEAD detached where it

133
00:09:10,102 --> 00:09:15,357
is. That's what deleting the directory by
hand does, except that --quit does it on

134
00:09:15,397 --> 00:09:20,781
purpose. So that's your colleague's second
question: no, don't delete what is stuck.

135
00:09:20,821 --> 00:09:21,169
Abort.

136
00:09:21,509 --> 00:09:26,788
When a rebase runs to the end, the two
reflogs record it differently, as you saw in

137
00:09:26,828 --> 00:09:30,249
the last video. The HEAD
reflog has one line per step.

138
00:09:30,289 --> 00:09:35,441
The branch reflog has one line for the
whole rebase, and the line below it is the

139
00:09:35,481 --> 00:09:40,761
old tip. So <branch>@{1} means "this branch
before its last rebase", and that's the

140
00:09:40,801 --> 00:09:43,004
most reliable undo handle you have.

141
00:09:43,344 --> 00:09:47,534
Step 8: the other backend.
labs/run ch09/apply-backend.

142
00:09:47,574 --> 00:09:53,457
Until Git 2.26 the default implementation
turned each commit into a patch and

143
00:09:53,497 --> 00:09:58,148
applied the patches. It's still
there, as git rebase --apply.

144
00:09:58,488 --> 00:10:04,061
Different words: "rewinding head",
"Applying", "Patch failed at 0002".

145
00:10:04,401 --> 00:10:09,227
A different state directory, .git/rebase-apply,
with one numbered patch file per

146
00:10:09,267 --> 00:10:12,755
commit. "Rebase in progress"
without the word interactive.

147
00:10:12,795 --> 00:10:17,500
And a conflict label that carries only
the subject of your commit, not its ID.

148
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:19,479
The way out is the same command.

149
00:10:19,819 --> 00:10:24,703
The manual lists what the apply backend
does worse: it works from patch context and

150
00:10:24,743 --> 00:10:29,508
can apply a hunk in the wrong place without
reporting a conflict, it can't detect

151
00:10:29,548 --> 00:10:34,431
directory renames, and it drops empty
commits. The textbook's advice is four words:

152
00:10:34,471 --> 00:10:36,033
leave rebase.backend alone.

153
00:10:37,873 --> 00:10:39,521
Five mistakes to watch for.

154
00:10:39,861 --> 00:10:43,401
Believing the branch has already
moved when a rebase stops.

155
00:10:43,441 --> 00:10:48,377
Root cause: the branch ref is touched only
when the todo list is empty; until then

156
00:10:48,417 --> 00:10:51,351
HEAD is detached and the
branch equals ORIG_HEAD.

157
00:10:51,691 --> 00:10:56,301
Deleting .git/rebase-merge to "get
out". Root cause: the directory is the

158
00:10:56,341 --> 00:11:01,653
interpreter's state; removing it leaves HEAD
detached on a half-built history, which

159
00:11:01,693 --> 00:11:03,755
is what --quit does deliberately.

160
00:11:04,095 --> 00:11:08,560
Panicking at "interactive rebase in
progress". Root cause: the default backend

161
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:13,411
always writes the marker file interactive,
and git status reports the file, not what

162
00:11:13,451 --> 00:11:13,988
you typed.

163
00:11:14,328 --> 00:11:19,512
Reading ORIG_HEAD or REBASE_HEAD with
cat. Root cause: since Git 2.46 they are

164
00:11:19,552 --> 00:11:25,138
ordinary root refs, not pseudorefs, and
their storage depends on the ref format; git

165
00:11:25,178 --> 00:11:27,214
rev-parse reads them correctly.

166
00:11:27,554 --> 00:11:31,090
Aborting after an hour of conflict
resolution to "start clean".

167
00:11:31,130 --> 00:11:35,460
Root cause: --abort resets the working
tree and the index to the old tip, and

168
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:38,071
unstaged resolution edits
are objects nowhere.

169
00:11:39,911 --> 00:11:44,801
Now, out of the lab. A shared build machine
for nightly evaluations starts failing

170
00:11:44,841 --> 00:11:49,611
with a strange message about a detached
HEAD. Someone logs in and finds that the

171
00:11:49,651 --> 00:11:54,600
workspace was left in the middle of a rebase
by an interrupted job, three days ago.

172
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:59,736
The textbook's routine for this is
short. "Is a rebase in progress here?"

173
00:11:59,776 --> 00:12:04,703
The first line of git status answers
it, and so does ls .git | grep rebase.

174
00:12:04,743 --> 00:12:10,135
If the answer is yes, nothing is lost: the
branch ref hasn't moved, and git rebase

175
00:12:10,175 --> 00:12:14,374
--abort returns to it. Don't delete
the state directory by hand.

176
00:12:14,714 --> 00:12:19,514
The second lesson from that incident is
for the pipeline, not for the person: an

177
00:12:19,554 --> 00:12:24,475
automated job that rebases must handle a
non-zero exit status by aborting, so that

178
00:12:24,515 --> 00:12:28,468
it never leaves a state directory
behind for the next job to find.

179
00:12:30,308 --> 00:12:37,520
Your turn. Do Exercise 9.3, Level 1, "a
conflict, a look around, and the way back",

180
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:40,665
in exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

181
00:12:41,005 --> 00:12:45,907
When the rebase stops, don't resolve
anything. First write down your predictions

182
00:12:45,947 --> 00:12:46,154
for:

183
00:12:46,494 --> 00:12:48,393
The content of .git/HEAD.

184
00:12:48,733 --> 00:12:52,324
The four values: HEAD, the
branch, ORIG_HEAD, REBASE_HEAD.

185
00:12:52,364 --> 00:12:53,826
Which of them are equal?

186
00:12:54,166 --> 00:12:56,293
The content of done and
of git-rebase-todo.

187
00:12:56,633 --> 00:12:59,819
Then check each one with a
command, and only then leave.

188
00:13:00,159 --> 00:13:06,137
The challenge is Exercise 9.7, Level 3, "a
rebase that stopped at an exec line", in

189
00:13:06,177 --> 00:13:07,152
the same file.

190
00:13:08,992 --> 00:13:15,199
Q147: "A rebase is stopped at a conflict.
Where do the branch ref, HEAD, ORIG_HEAD

191
00:13:15,239 --> 00:13:19,541
and REBASE_HEAD point? What
does that imply for --abort?"

192
00:13:21,881 --> 00:13:27,026
Answer out loud first. A strong answer gives
four positions without hesitation, and

193
00:13:27,066 --> 00:13:32,085
says which two are equal and why. It then
derives the second half from the first:

194
00:13:32,125 --> 00:13:35,957
what --abort has to do, and what
it therefore can't give back.

195
00:13:35,997 --> 00:13:39,580
It mentions where the information
for the abort is stored.

196
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:44,764
If the follow-up asks what happens to a
commit you made by hand during the stop, be

197
00:13:44,804 --> 00:13:47,263
ready to reason from "HEAD is detached".

198
00:13:49,103 --> 00:13:52,030
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

199
00:13:52,370 --> 00:13:57,774
A rebase writes a todo list into
.git/rebase-merge, runs it on a detached HEAD, and

200
00:13:57,814 --> 00:14:03,153
moves the branch only when the list is
empty. While it's stopped, the branch still

201
00:14:03,193 --> 00:14:08,532
equals ORIG_HEAD, HEAD is on the last copy,
and REBASE_HEAD names the commit being

202
00:14:08,572 --> 00:14:09,122
replayed.

203
00:14:09,462 --> 00:14:13,720
git status reads the state directory
back: done, remaining, and the ways out.

204
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:16,867
--abort returns to the branch and
discards resolution work in progress.

205
00:14:16,907 --> 00:14:18,872
--quit removes the state
and leaves HEAD detached.

206
00:14:19,212 --> 00:14:24,484
The apply backend uses .git/rebase-apply
and patch files, and the default merge

207
00:14:24,524 --> 00:14:26,300
backend is the one to keep.

208
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:30,651
Read section 9.4 of Chapter 9.

209
00:14:30,991 --> 00:14:34,048
A stopped rebase used to
look like a broken branch.

